In fact, we do have the hypothesis that 2 of the groups are similar, but both differ from the third. I am not sure why this would be worse than doing a follow up in ANOVA where the omnibus F is significant, and you do t tests to see how the groups differ. We’d just be doing one contrast (2 vs 1) to follow up an a priori hypothesis. It would seem to be a better way than collapsing the two groups together and assuming they are invariant.

